<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Active aging hydration principles"><div><strong>Risk can rise with age</strong><span>Thirst, renal concentrating ability, total body water and practical access can change across later life.</span></div><div><strong>Barriers are often practical</strong><span>Mobility, cognition, swallowing, continence concerns and dependence on others can reduce intake.</span></div><div><strong>More is not always better</strong><span>Heart, kidney and other conditions may require individualized fluid or electrolyte limits.</span></div></div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature"><div><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Hydration across later life</p><h2>Older adults are diverse, so hydration advice should be too</h2><p>Research consistently identifies dehydration as a concern in older populations, particularly in hospitals, long-term care and people with greater dependency. Age-related physiology can reduce the margin for fluid imbalance, while medications, illness and functional barriers can add complexity.</p><p>At the same time, newer research cautions against turning this into a simplistic instruction that every older adult must force large amounts of water before thirst. Healthy active adults, frail residents and people with heart or kidney disease may need very different strategies.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__split"><div class="bhd-page__split-media"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/8307327/pexels-photo-8307327.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Mature woman practicing balance exercise indoors while holding a water bottle" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="bhd-page__split-copy"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Support independence</p><h2>Design the routine around what the person can actually do</h2><p>For an independent older adult, that may mean carrying water during walking, exercise or gardening and drinking with meals. For someone with mobility or cognitive limitations, it may mean visible drinks, preferred cups, regular offers, assistance and monitoring.</p><p>The goal is not just to provide fluid. It is to reduce the friction between the person and safe, appropriate intake.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Common aging-related considerations</p><h2>Look beyond thirst alone</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4"><article><h3>Physiology</h3><p>Age-related changes in thirst and renal water handling can alter response to heat, exercise and low intake.</p></article><article><h3>Function</h3><p>Mobility, vision, dexterity and cognition can affect the ability to obtain and prepare drinks.</p></article><article><h3>Medications and disease</h3><p>Diuretics and conditions affecting heart, kidney or endocrine function can change fluid and electrolyte management.</p></article><article><h3>Environment</h3><p>Heat, exercise, illness and care-setting routines can increase vulnerability or make access more difficult.</p></article></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid"><article class="bhd-page__prose"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Where Best Hydrate may fit</p><h2>A beverage option for appropriate adults, not a universal geriatric prescription</h2><p>Best Hydrate may be an option for an older adult who is permitted the product's ingredients and fluid volume, can swallow it safely and prefers a flavoured electrolyte drink during activity, heat or routine use.</p><p>It should not be promoted as automatically superior to water for all older adults. People with prescribed fluid, sodium, potassium or carbohydrate restrictions should follow their healthcare plan.</p></article><aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Individualize</span><h3>Healthy 65 is not the same as medically complex 85.</h3><p>Age alone is not enough information to prescribe a beverage or fluid volume.</p></aside></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Stay active</p><h2>Hydration should support the life being lived</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3"><article><h3>Active Living</h3><p>Walking, cycling, classes and outdoor recreation often need simple, portable hydration.</p><a href="/pages/active-living">Active Living →</a></article><article><h3>Heat & High Sweat</h3><p>Hot weather raises concern for anyone exercising or working outside.</p><a href="/pages/heat-high-sweat">Heat & High Sweat →</a></article><article><h3>Recovery & Rehabilitation</h3><p>Illness or injury can add access, swallowing and care-plan barriers.</p><a href="/pages/recovery-rehabilitation">Recovery & Rehabilitation →</a></article></div></section>

